  "answer": "In a food chain, the amount of energy transferred from one step to the next decreases. This is because only a small proportion of the energy from one trophic level is passed on to the next. For example, when animals eat, only a small part of their food is converted into new tissue, which forms the food for the next trophic level. The rest of the energy is used for the animal's life processes, such as staying alive, or is lost as heat or waste. Typically, only about 10% of the energy from one level is transferred to the next, with the majority being lost as heat or through metabolic processes.",
